# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <vxui.h>

vx_app *vx_app_open(const char *id);
const char *vx_app_error(const vx_app *app);
bool vx_wait(vx_app *app, vx_event *ev, vx_instant deadline);
void vx_app_wake(vx_app *app);

vx_window *vx_window_open(vx_app *app, const char *title,
                          uint32_t width, uint32_t height);
void vx_window_redraw(vx_window *win);
void vx_window_animate(vx_window *win, bool on);
void vx_window_title(vx_window *win, const char *title);
void vx_window_text_input(vx_window *win, const char *purpose);

vx_canvas *vx_canvas_begin(vx_window *win, const vx_frame_event *frame);
void vx_clear(vx_canvas *c, vx_color colour);
void vx_fill_rect(vx_canvas *c, float x, float y, float w, float h,
                  vx_color colour);
void vx_circle(vx_canvas *c, float x, float y, float r, vx_color colour);
void vx_canvas_present(vx_canvas *c);

vx_pixels vx_pixels_begin(vx_window *win, const vx_frame_event *frame);
void vx_pixels_present(vx_window *win, vx_pixels *px);

vx_ui *vx_ui_begin(vx_window *win, const vx_frame_event *frame);
void vx_ui_end(vx_ui *ui);
void vx_label(vx_ui *ui, const char *text);
bool vx_button(vx_ui *ui, const char *label);
void vx_push_id(vx_ui *ui, uint64_t key);
void vx_pop_id(vx_ui *ui);

vx_voice *vx_voice_open(vx_app *app, vx_sound sound);
void vx_voice_play(vx_voice *voice);
```

# DESCRIPTION

vxui is the app tier (03 §6), v0 (M7 step 7e2).
An app of the SDK (native(7)) includes `<vxui.h>` and links `/lib/libvxui.so` and libvx.so (ADR-0056); the system's own programs compile vxui in, as they compile libvx in.
An app has one loop and one wait, its own: `vx_wait` returns the next event of any of its windows in libvx's `vx_event` (loop(2)), with vxui's kinds, the window as `source`: `VX_FRAME` (`frame`: its sequence, `target`, `prev_presented` and `dt`), `VX_KEY` (`keyboard`: the HID usage, its unmodified rune, the modifiers, down and repeat), `VX_POINTER` (`pointer`: x and y in the window, the buttons, the wheel), `VX_TEXT` (`text`, good until the queue has gone round, 32 events later), `VX_CONFIGURE` (`configure`: the size, focus), `VX_CLOSE` and `VX_WAKE`.
A window's first event is a `VX_CONFIGURE`, its size and focus as it opens.
`vx_app_wake`, from any thread, makes the app's `vx_wait` return a `VX_WAKE` (one for any number of wakes since the last), so a thread of the app's own, a reader, can hand it work.
With a deadline that comes first, it returns `true` and `VX_NONE`.
The toolkit never calls back.

A window is made by opening `/wsys/new`, the window server's app attach (winsrv(8); a manifest gives it with `mount=/wsys srv=wsys aname=self`), titled and sized with its `ctl`, and drawn into two buffers in turn.
`vx_window_text_input` turns on the window's text input (winsrv(8)'s `ime` file): text the server resolved, through dead keys, compose or an input method, comes as `VX_TEXT`, and keys still come as `VX_KEY` for commands; the purpose is `text`, `password`, `number`, `url`, `email` or `terminal`, and a null one turns it off, as a window starts.

It draws only when asked: `vx_window_redraw` asks for one `VX_FRAME`, `vx_window_animate` for one every frame the output shows; either comes only with a credit of the frame protocol in hand, so an idle window costs nothing.

`vx_canvas_begin` and `vx_canvas_present` bracket drawing with `vx_clear`, `vx_fill_rect` and `vx_circle` (antialiased); colours are XRGB or the theme's (`VX_THEME_BG`, `VX_THEME_FG`, `VX_THEME_ACCENT`, `VX_THEME_FACE`).
`vx_pixels_begin` gives the buffer itself (`data`, `w`, `h`, `stride`) and its `age`, the presents since its contents were last shown (0 for a new one); `vx_pixels_present` presents it.

The UI is immediate-mode (M7 step 7e2b): each frame it draws, the app describes it between `vx_ui_begin` and `vx_ui_end`, and holds no widget objects.
`vx_label` and `vx_button` add a widget; `vx_ui_end` lays them out (a column), finds the damage by comparing the frame's widgets with the last frame's, and draws only that, or presents nothing when nothing changed.
A widget's id is its key's hash seeded with its parent's: the key is the label, or the whole string when it holds `##` (`"Delete##row17"` shows "Delete"); `vx_push_id` and `vx_pop_id` seed the widgets between them, for rows made in a loop.
The ids key the cache kept between frames (each widget's rectangle and state); a widget not described in a frame is dropped from it.
Input is matched against the rectangles of the frame the user saw: `vx_button` is `true` once, on the frame after a press and its release both landed on it.
A window with a UI is asked to draw when its pointer moves or its buttons change.
Text is Inter, 13 pixels.

Errors are sticky: a failed open makes the next `vx_wait` return `false`, and `vx_app_error` says why, so a program need not check each call.

Hot reload (03 §6.1, M7 step 7g2b) is a convention, not a mechanism: during development an app is a small host and a code image, `app.so`, that exports `vx_app_update(vx_app_memory *mem, vx_event *ev)`. The host maps `mem` at the same address every run (`vx_as_reserve` with `VX_AS_FIXED`, a lazy VMO), opens the window, and calls the image's `vx_app_update` with each event. All of the app's state is in `mem->storage`; `mem->app` and `mem->window` are the host's. A thread watches the image's directory (`vx_watch`) and calls `vx_app_wake` when a new `app.so` is renamed into place; on the `VX_WAKE` the host maps it with `vx_image_open` (image(2)) and calls it from the next event, counting `mem->reloads`. Old images stay mapped, so a pointer in storage into one stays good; each image's own static data starts afresh. A build writes `app.so.new` and renames it, so the host never reads half a file.

Voices are numbered now and sound with audiod (M13): `vx_voice_open` gives one, `vx_voice_play` is silent until then.

# EXAMPLES

03 §6's minimal program, in ten calls, is tests/user/minimal.c; its CPU pixel program is tests/user/pixels.c, and tests/user/pixelsdyn.c the same as an SDK app on libvxui.so; tests/user/uitest.c uses the UI. The hot-reload host's template is tests/user/reloadhost.c, an app the host runs tests/user/reload_app.c.

